            We compared every common type of chainsaw sharpener: plain file kits, handheld file guides, bar-mounted jigs, 12-volt and rotary tool grinders, and bench grinders. Then we picked the best example of each type, because the right sharpener depends much more on how you use your saw than on any single feature.
          </p>

          <p>
            We weighted three things. Accuracy, meaning how consistently the tool holds the right angle and cutter length. Depth gauge handling, because a sharpener that ignores the depth gauges only does half the job. And fit, meaning whether it suits the chain pitches and saws people actually own, from a 12-inch battery saw to a 20-inch gas saw.
          </p>

          <p>
            Specifications come from the manufacturers and retail listings, and star ratings are Amazon figures at the time of writing, rounded down. We left out products sold only from their own websites, products we could not verify, and several cheap unbranded electric sharpeners whose specifications did not hold up. Prices change too often to quote, so we describe value in words.
          </p>

          <p>
            One point before the picks: a sharpener does not replace a chain forever. Chains wear out, and a chain that has hit rocks, nails or dirt repeatedly may be cheaper to replace than to restore. We explain how to tell the difference below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
