          </h2>

          <p>
            A round file in a handle is the simplest sharpener and the one professional fallers use most. It is cheap, works anywhere and removes very little metal, so chains last longer. The difficulty is holding the angle and depth consistently by hand, which is what file guides and jigs exist to fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Handheld file guides, like STIHL's 2-in-1 Easy File, sit on the chain and hold the files at the right height and angle. Bar-mounted jigs, like Granberg's File-N-Joint, clamp to the bar and carry the file on a rigid carriage for even more precision. Both still use a file, so they are quiet, portable and gentle on the chain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Powered sharpeners are faster. Handheld 12-volt grinders and rotary tool kits restore an edge in seconds per cutter and can be used on the saw. Bench grinders clamp the chain in a vise and grind every cutter to identical angles, which makes them the best tool for restoring a badly damaged chain or sharpening many chains at once.
          </p>

