              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Treat that table as a starting point, not a rule. File size can vary with the chain series and the manufacturer, so always confirm against the chain maker's chart. Oregon's field kit includes one, and it is the most useful piece of paper in the box. A file that is too small cuts a hook into the cutter; one that is too large leaves the edge blunt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gauge does not change the file size, but it matters when you buy replacement chain and when you clamp a chain in a grinder vise. If you own several saws, keep a note of each saw's pitch, gauge and drive link count on the inside of the case lid. It saves a lot of guessing at the store.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="depth-gauges" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Depth Gauges: The Half of Sharpening People Skip
          </h2>

          <p>
            Each cutter on a chain has a small hump in front of it called the depth gauge, or raker. It controls how deeply the cutter bites. As you sharpen, the cutter gets shorter and lower, while the depth gauge stays the same height, so after several sharpenings the gauge prevents the cutter from reaching the wood at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why a freshly filed chain can still cut slowly and produce dust instead of chips. The fix is to lower the depth gauges with a flat file, using a depth gauge tool that sits over the cutter and exposes only the part of the gauge that needs removing. The correct setting is specified by the chain maker and is commonly around 0.025 inch for many standard chains, smaller on some low-kickback chains.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not overdo it. Depth gauges that are too low make the chain grab, vibrate and kick back more readily, which is a safety problem, not just a cutting one. Lower them only a little at a time, and round the front edge afterward to keep the original profile.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is where STIHL's 2-in-1 file guide earns its top spot. It files the cutter and the depth gauge in the same stroke, so the two always stay in proportion. Bar-mounted jigs and bench grinders also handle depth gauges, but you have to remember to do it. Most hand-filing kits rely on you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="angles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Top-Plate, Side-Plate and Down Angles
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three angles define a sharpened cutter. The top-plate angle is the angle of the file across the chain, viewed from above. Most standard chisel and semi-chisel chains use around 30 degrees, though some low-kickback and specialty chains call for 25 or 35, and ripping chain uses a much shallower angle.
          </p>

          <p>
            The down angle, or tilt of the file, is usually held level or tilted slightly downward depending on the chain, and the side-plate angle is mostly set by holding the file at the correct height in the cutter. File guides and the witness marks stamped on many cutters make these easier to get right, so look for them before you start.
          </p>

          <p>
            Consistency matters more than perfection. A chain with every cutter at 28 degrees will cut straighter than one with cutters ranging from 25 to 35. Inconsistent angles, and especially inconsistent cutter lengths from side to side, are why chains pull to one side and cut a curve.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bench grinders and bar-mounted jigs excel here because the angle is set mechanically and repeated for every tooth. If you file by hand, use a guide with angle lines, keep the file at the same height every stroke, and sharpen all the cutters on one side before turning the saw around to do the other.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to File a Chain Properly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Secure the saw first. Clamp the bar in a vise, set the chain brake, and on a gas saw remove the spark plug wire; on a battery saw remove the pack. A chain that moves while you file will never sharpen evenly. If you are working at a bench, our guide to the <a href="/best-bench-vise/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best bench vises</a> covers vises that grip a bar securely, and a stump vise does the same job in the field.
          </p>

          <p>
            Find the shortest cutter on the chain and use it as your reference; every cutter needs to end up the same length. File from the inside of the cutter toward the outside, pushing the file forward with steady, even pressure and lifting it on the return stroke. Rotate the file slightly as you go so it wears evenly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Count your strokes, typically three to five per cutter, and use the same number on every one. Mark the starting cutter with a paint pen so you know where you began. Sharpen all the cutters facing one way, then move to the other side of the saw and do the rest.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finish with the depth gauges, then check your work. A sharp cutter has a bright, clean edge with no rounded corner, and it should feel grabby under a fingernail. Test cut a log: a sharp chain pulls itself into the wood and throws coarse chips, not fine dust. If the cut wanders, check that the cutters on each side are the same length.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-to-sharpen" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. When to Sharpen and When to Replace
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sharpen when the saw starts telling you. The signs are fine sawdust instead of chips, needing to push the saw into the cut, smoke from the cut, or the saw pulling to one side. A light touch-up with a file every tank of fuel or battery charge keeps a chain cutting far better than waiting until it is badly dull.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dirt, sand and rocks dull a chain faster than anything else. One contact with the ground can take the edge off every cutter that touched it. Keep the bar out of the dirt, clean the bark of muddy logs before cutting, and expect to sharpen immediately after hitting anything hard.
          </p>

          <p>
            Replace the chain when the cutters have been filed back to the minimum length marked on the top plate, when cutters are cracked or broken, or when the chain has stretched so much that it can no longer be tensioned. A bench grinder can rescue a chain with damaged cutters, but if the damage is deep, a new chain is often the cheaper option.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sharpening schedules work the same way for other blades around the yard: little and often beats occasional heavy grinding. Our guide to <a href="/how-often-to-sharpen-mower-blades/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">how often to sharpen mower blades</a> follows the same logic. If you burn through chains cutting firewood, pairing a sharp saw with a good <a href="/best-log-splitter/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">log splitter</a> is the quickest way to cut the work in half.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="grinders" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Using a Bench Grinder Without Ruining a Chain
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bench grinders are the most accurate way to sharpen a chain, and the easiest way to ruin one. The danger is heat. Hold the wheel against a cutter for too long and the steel turns blue, which means it has lost its hardness and will dull almost immediately. Use quick, light touches, and let the cutter cool if it gets warm.
          </p>

          <p>
            Match the wheel to the chain. Oregon's grinders come with 3.2 mm and 4.7 mm wheels for different cutter sizes, and the wheel thickness must suit your pitch. Dress the wheel with the dressing brick whenever it glazes or loses its profile, because a worn wheel grinds the wrong shape into every cutter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Set the angles from the chain maker's specification, not from memory, and use the grinder's stop so every cutter is ground to the same length. Grind all the cutters on one side, then reset the vise angle and do the other side. Finish the depth gauges with the flat disc or a hand file.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wear safety glasses and keep hands clear of the wheel. A bench grinder for chains is a different tool from a general-purpose grinder or an <a href="/best-angle-grinder/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">angle grinder</a>, and you should never freehand a chain on either of those. The vise and stops on a dedicated chain grinder are what make the result accurate and safe.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pole-and-battery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Sharpening Small Battery and Pole Saw Chains
          </h2>

          <p>
            Small battery chainsaws and pole saws often use 3/8-inch low-profile or 1/4-inch chain, which has smaller cutters than a typical gas saw and needs a smaller file, usually 5/32 inch. Using a .325-inch or 3/8-inch file on these chains is a common mistake that ruins the cutter shape.
          </p>

          <p>
            These chains are also thinner and narrower, so they dull faster when they touch dirt and lose more of their life with each heavy sharpening. Light, frequent touch-ups with a correctly sized file are the best approach. Low-profile chains often have built-in low-kickback features, so follow the maker's depth gauge setting closely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pole saw chains are particularly worth keeping sharp, because a dull chain makes you push, and pushing a saw 12 feet overhead is tiring and inaccurate. If you prune with a pole saw, see our guide to the <a href="/best-pole-saw/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best pole saws</a> for the chain sizes the popular models use, and keep the right file in the kit with the saw.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some battery saw owners prefer to swap chains rather than sharpen in the field, carrying a spare loop and sharpening both at home. That is a sensible approach: a chain takes seconds to change on most saws with tool-free tensioning, and you can sharpen properly at a bench with a vise rather than on your knee. Our <a href="/best-chainsaw/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">chainsaw buyer's guide</a> notes which saws make chain changes easy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the Wrong File Size</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A file that is too small hooks the cutter; one that is too large leaves it blunt. Check the pitch stamped on the drive links and confirm the file size against the chain maker's chart. Small battery and pole saw chains usually need a smaller file than gas saws.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Depth Gauges</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sharpening the cutters without lowering the depth gauges leaves a chain that looks sharp but makes dust instead of chips. Check the gauges every few sharpenings with a depth gauge tool and lower them to the maker's setting, then round the front edge. Go slowly, because gauges filed too low make the chain grab and kick.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Uneven Cutter Lengths</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Filing one side harder than the other makes the chain pull and cut a curve. Use the shortest cutter as your reference, count strokes, and keep every cutter the same length. A bar-mounted jig or bench grinder makes this much easier than freehand filing, and a paint mark on the first cutter stops you losing count.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overheating Cutters on a Grinder</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Holding a grinding wheel or rotary stone on a cutter too long turns the steel blue and soft, and the edge will not last. Use quick, light touches, keep the wheel dressed, and let the chain cool. A blue cutter needs grinding back past the damage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sharpening a Chain That Should Be Replaced</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Once cutters reach the minimum length marked on the top plate, or are cracked or badly chipped, sharpening wastes time. Replace the chain instead. Keep a spare loop with the saw so you can swap it in the field when one goes dull, then sharpen the old chain properly at home with a vise and good light.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best chainsaw sharpener for beginners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A handheld file guide that also lowers the depth gauges, like STIHL's 2-in-1 Easy File, is the easiest to get right because the guide sets the angle and depth for you. An Oregon field kit is the cheapest place to start if you have several chain sizes, since it includes three file sizes and a filing chart.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it better to file or grind a chainsaw chain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both work. Hand filing removes less metal, works anywhere and is what most professionals do between tanks. Bench grinding is faster and more consistent, and it is the best way to restore a damaged chain. Many owners file in the field and grind once or twice a season to bring the chain back to true.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size file do I need for my chainsaw chain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the chain pitch. As a rough guide, 3/8-inch low-profile and 1/4-inch chains use a 5/32-inch file, .325-inch uses 3/16-inch, and full 3/8-inch uses 13/64 or 7/32-inch. Always confirm with the chain maker's chart, because file size can vary by chain series, and the pitch is usually stamped on the drive links or printed on the chain box.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I sharpen a chainsaw chain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Touch it up whenever it starts making dust instead of chips, needs pushing, or pulls to one side, which for many users is every tank of fuel or battery charge. Hitting dirt or a rock dulls it instantly. Light, frequent filing keeps it cutting better and lasting longer than waiting until it is very dull.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What angle should I sharpen a chainsaw chain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most standard chisel and semi-chisel chains use a top-plate angle of around 30 degrees, though some low-kickback and specialty chains specify 25 or 35 degrees, and ripping chain is much shallower. Check the witness marks on the cutters or the chain maker's specification, and keep the angle consistent on every cutter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are electric chainsaw sharpeners worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A bench grinder is worth it if you run several chains or cut a lot of firewood, because it sharpens quickly and consistently. Handheld 12V and rotary tool sharpeners are fast for field touch-ups but easy to overheat or overgrind. For one saw used occasionally, a good file guide is all you need.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many times can a chainsaw chain be sharpened?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many chains can be sharpened ten times or more, depending on how much metal each sharpening removes. Light filing extends chain life, while heavy grinding to repair damage shortens it. Replace the chain when cutters reach the minimum length marked on the top plate, or when they are cracked or broken.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

