        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Match the sharpener to the way you cut. One or two saws on the same chain pitch: buy a STIHL 2-in-1 file guide in the right size and keep it with the saw. Several saws or a lot of firewood: add an Oregon bench grinder and true the chains up once or twice a season.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you choose, the fundamentals matter more than the tool: the right file size for your pitch, the same angle and cutter length on every tooth, and depth gauges kept at the maker's setting. Do those three things and any sharpener on this list will keep your saw cutting like new.
          </p>
